{"authored" [{"question" "q-9608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{10}.$ Give your answer to $5$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=10$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{10}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(10-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{216} \\\\  & =3.162\\,04,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-9608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{10}.$ Give your answer to $5$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=10$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{10}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(10-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{216} \\\\  & =3.162\\,04,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "84c640f525ff2121be056a3041b2e17ad9d3cc0349bf43c60e36f5025a3f6886"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{10}≈3.161\\,12"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}≈3.161\\,97"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}≈3.162\\,04"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}≈3.164\\,39"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}≈3.165\\,12"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{10}≈3.162\\,04"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-83187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ centered about the point $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-83187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{(5)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(5)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0+(-1)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{0}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2} \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{0}{4!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+\\frac{(-1)}{5!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯ \\\\  & =-(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{6}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}-\\frac{1}{120}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-83187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "84c640f525ff2121be056a3041b2e17ad9d3cc0349bf43c60e36f5025a3f6886"} {"question" "q-83187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-83187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{12}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}-\\frac{1}{240}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{18}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}-\\frac{1}{240}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}-\\frac{1}{60}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{6}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}-\\frac{1}{120}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}-\\frac{1}{27}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-83187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{6}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}-\\frac{1}{120}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-126844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ about the point $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-126844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ and $a=2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ f(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+(\\frac{1}{2})(x-2)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{4})(x-2)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-126844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "84c640f525ff2121be056a3041b2e17ad9d3cc0349bf43c60e36f5025a3f6886"} {"question" "q-126844" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-126844" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+(x-2)-\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{4}(x-2)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-(x-2)+\\frac{1}{4}(x-2)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{4}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-126844" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+⋯"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-347614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ about the point $x=2.$\n\n$\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=${{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-2)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-2)^{2}-⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-347614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and $a=2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}={x}^{-2} \\\\ f(2) & ={2}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}=-2{x}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-2(2)^{-3}=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}=6{x}^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =6(2)^{-4}=\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{4})(x-2)+\\frac{(\\frac{3}{8})}{2!}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{4})}{3!}(x-2)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}(x-2)+\\frac{3}{16}(x-2)^{2}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-347614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "84c640f525ff2121be056a3041b2e17ad9d3cc0349bf43c60e36f5025a3f6886"} {"question" "q-347614" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-347614" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-347614" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-347614" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-347614" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-347614" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{16}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-354043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ to approximate the value of $cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-354043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $a=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =-{csc}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-1+(-2)(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+⋯ \\\\  & =-1-2(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2$ into the above to approximate $cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2) & ≈-1-2(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-1-2(0.2) \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-354043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "84c640f525ff2121be056a3041b2e17ad9d3cc0349bf43c60e36f5025a3f6886"} {"question" "q-354043" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064659/state.json" "file_sha256" "533ebd405ba51384ff09a8e77898a304e0f0b546ab5d3fe183817321e960e3a8"} {"question" "q-354043" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/refe